WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.829
Authentication answers the question, who
is making this request.

2
00:00:05.909 --> 00:00:10.761
Authorization answers, what is that
identity allowed to do.

3
00:00:10.841 --> 00:00:16.265
Flask applications often combine login
forms, password handling,

4
00:00:16.345 --> 00:00:22.625
session cookies, password reset flows, API
tokens, SSO, or MFA integrations.

5
00:00:22.705 --> 00:00:26.987
A secure design keeps those
responsibilities clear.

6
00:00:27.067 --> 00:00:33.442
Successful authentication should establish
an identity and session context,

7
00:00:33.522 --> 00:00:39.707
but it should not automatically grant
access to every route, record, tenant,

8
00:00:39.787 --> 00:00:41.976
or administrative action.

9
00:00:42.056 --> 00:00:49.002
Password handling should rely on
established password hashing and identity patterns

10
00:00:49.082 --> 00:00:53.268
rather than homegrown storage or
comparison logic.

11
00:00:53.348 --> 00:00:58.962
MFA integration should be treated as part
of the authentication flow,

12
00:00:59.042 --> 00:01:04.847
with attention to enrollment, recovery,
reset, and step-up decisions.

13
00:01:04.927 --> 00:01:11.587
Account recovery is especially important
because a weak reset path can undermine a

14
00:01:11.667 --> 00:01:13.190
strong login path.

15
00:01:13.270 --> 00:01:16.410
The goal is not to memorize one library,

16
00:01:16.490 --> 00:01:22.580
but to understand which parts of the flow
need protection and verification.

17
00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:27.703
Flask sessions commonly rely on signed
cookies at a high level,

18
00:01:27.783 --> 00:01:33.682
meaning the client may hold session data
protected against tampering by a

19
00:01:33.762 --> 00:01:35.475
server-side secret.

20
00:01:35.555 --> 00:01:42.406
Signing is not the same as
confidentiality, and session content should be minimized.

21
00:01:42.486 --> 00:01:46.673
Do not store sensitive records, broad
permissions,

22
00:01:46.753 --> 00:01:50.273
or large state in a client-carried
session.

23
00:01:50.353 --> 00:01:54.730
Server-side session stores can reduce some
exposure,

24
00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:58.236
but they add lifecycle, storage, cleanup,

25
00:01:58.316 --> 00:02:02.978
and availability considerations that still
need design.

26
00:02:03.058 --> 00:02:07.721
Cookies need deliberate attributes and
lifecycle rules.

27
00:02:07.801 --> 00:02:14.557
Secure transport, HTTP-only behavior,
same-site behavior, expiration, renewal,

28
00:02:14.637 --> 00:02:17.586
and logout handling all affect risk.

29
00:02:17.666 --> 00:02:24.137
Session fixation concerns appear when an
attacker-controlled or stale session

30
00:02:24.217 --> 00:02:27.166
survives login or privilege change,

31
00:02:27.246 --> 00:02:33.146
so applications should refresh session
state at important transitions.

32
00:02:33.226 --> 00:02:39.982
Logout should invalidate the relevant
server-side state where applicable and make

33
00:02:40.062 --> 00:02:42.250
the client state unusable.

34
00:02:42.330 --> 00:02:48.420
Sessions are small security contracts, and
they should be treated that way.
